WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
As many as 37 percent
of breast cancer cases may be

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
attributable to exposure
to bovine leukemia virus,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.110
which I first did a
series about years ago.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:13.080
In this two-part series,
I review the science

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:17.439
behind this phenomenon and update
it with the more recent data.

00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:22.422
To see the original series, check
out the doctor’s note for this video.

00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:31.975
"Bovine Leukemia Virus as
a Cause of Breast Cancer"

00:00:32.169 --> 00:00:36.570
The incidence of breast cancer is
continuously increasing worldwide.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.079
In the U.S., this amounted
to a 40 percent increase

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:41.870
in the incidence by the
turn of this century.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.949
Currently, the main approach is
early detection and treatment.

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:48.269
That’s important, but why not pay
more attention to primary prevention;

00:00:48.269 --> 00:00:50.680
in other words, protecting
people from being exposed to

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.609
risk factors for breast cancer
so that they never develop

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:56.269
breast cancer in the ﬁrst place.

00:00:56.269 --> 00:00:59.100
“Overall, it is estimated that
20 percent of all human cancers

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.550
have an infectious origin.”

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.710
Viruses can trigger cancer
by turning on cancer genes

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.630
or turning off cancer
suppressing genes,

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.680
but they can also
contribute to tumor formation

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.220
just by causing chronic inflammation.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.850
Currently, cancer-causing viruses
are considered the major

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:21.119
plausible hypothesis for a direct
cause of human breast cancer.

00:01:21.119 --> 00:01:22.380
How did we get here?

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:27.430
It all started about 40 years ago
when a professor of virology

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:32.969
at UC Berkeley learned how mouse
mammary tumor virus was discovered.

00:01:32.969 --> 00:01:36.320
Scientists swapped baby mouse pups
from mice with a high incidence

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.759
of mammary cancer with pups from
mouse strains with a low incidence

00:01:40.759 --> 00:01:44.479
and found that the cancer
incidence matched the foster mothers,

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:46.150
showing it wasn’t genetic.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.600
“It occurred to me,”
the professor thought,

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.470
“that humans are foster-
nursed on the cow.”

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.590
Bovine leukemia virus (BLV)
had just been identified as a

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.770
cancer-causing cow virus.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:01.079
At the time only about 1 in 10
U.S. dairy cows were infected,

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:02.960
but now it’s closer to half.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.240
We started out with
2/3s of herds affected.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.620
Then, it was more like 80 percent,
based on their milk

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:12.800
testing positive for the virus, and 100
percent of the larger industrial farms.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.830
And now, more than 9 out of 10
U.S. herds are affected,

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:20.500
a continuation of the
historical trend of the persistent

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.450
proliferation of BLV
within U.S. dairy herds.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:28.110
We’ve long known that countries
with the highest milk consumption

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.510
also had the highest
breast cancer incidence.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:34.060
And it’s not just a matchup
between dairy consumption

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.660
and breast cancer incidence
on the country level.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.720
Individual women who are lactose
intolerant and consume less dairy

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.290
also seem to have decreased
risk of breast cancer.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:47.840
Oh, but come on, there’s lots
of stuff in milk that could be

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.420
contributing to the cancer
risk like saturated fat and the

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.490
presence of cancer-promoting
growth hormones like IGF-1.

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.160
Yes, we know bovine leukemia virus

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.950
is present in marketed
beef and dairy products.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:03.510
About half of milk and meat samples
turn up positive for the virus.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.730
In fact, you can sample the virus
straight out of the air on dairy farms,

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.000
on surfaces, and in the milk itself.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.580
Most milk is pasteurized,
but many dairy products

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.680
like raw aged cheeses are not.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.270
And who hasn’t eaten a pink-
in-the-middle hamburger

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.495
at some point in their life?

00:03:20.495 --> 00:03:23.294
Yes, we have evidence that
people are exposed to the virus.

00:03:23.294 --> 00:03:27.090
Yes, we have evidence that people
are actively infected with the virus.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:31.110
But it wasn’t until 2015 that we
learned infection rates were highest

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:37.410
in cancerous breast tissue, so much
so that as many as 37 percent

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.580
of breast cancer cases may
be attributable to exposure

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.090
to the bovine leukemia virus.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:45.680
That was enough for me to
trigger a whole series of videos

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.920
on the role the virus
plays in breast cancer

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.450
and how the meat and dairy
industries responded to the news.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:55.890
Okay, now that we’re all back up
to speed, what’s the latest update?

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.785
That’s what I’ll cover next.

